WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.116
Fatigue is a performance constraint.

2
00:00:02.116 --> 00:00:08.466
It can reduce attention, slow reactions, weaken working memory, and make error detection less reliable.

3
00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:13.122
In technical work, that might mean missing a subtle configuration problem.

4
00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:16.508
In operations, it might mean skipping a check.

5
00:00:16.508 --> 00:00:23.280
In writing, it might mean failing to notice that the argument no longer supports the conclusion.

6
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.666
Fatigue can also affect emotional regulation and confidence.

7
00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:32.592
People may become more reactive, more impatient, or more certain than the evidence supports.

8
00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:38.941
Overconfidence under fatigue is especially risky because the person may not realize performance has degraded.

9
00:00:38.941 --> 00:00:46.137
They may continue making decisions with the same level of confidence while using weaker attention and judgment.

10
00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:55.026
Performance risk matters in driving, safety-sensitive roles, incident response, medical and operational settings, and any work where errors can compound.

11
00:00:55.026 --> 00:01:04.338
This course does not provide safety policy or medical guidance, but it does emphasize that recovery is part of responsible performance design.

12
00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:08.571
Teams should recognize when performance problems may be recovery problems.

13
00:01:08.571 --> 00:01:12.804
Persistent sleep, health, or safety concerns belong with qualified help.

14
00:01:12.804 --> 00:01:18.729
In learning and workplace design, the practical response is to treat fatigue as information.

15
00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:26.772
Adjust the task, add review, slow down when stakes are high, rotate roles when possible, or reschedule demanding work.

16
00:01:26.772 --> 00:01:32.698
Trying harder is not always the right answer when the system is under-recovered.
